WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.767
-Our next guest is a
Grammy-award winning musician,

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:04.834
one of the most successful
female acts ever,

00:00:04.834 --> 00:00:07.801
with over 80 million records
sold worldwide.

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:09.434
Her signature collection
of wines

00:00:09.434 --> 00:00:10.901
are now available to buy
in the U.S.

00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:13.400
Please welcome back to the show
Kylie Minogue, everyone!

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:25.067
♪♪

00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:29.934
♪♪

00:00:29.934 --> 00:00:30.934
-Hi, Kylie!
-Wow!

00:00:30.934 --> 00:00:32.033
-Thank you for being here,

00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:33.701
and thank you
for bringing some wine.

00:00:33.701 --> 00:00:36.434
Would you like
some of your rosé?

00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:37.667
-Yes.
-All right, great.

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:39.567
Here's a bottle,
a beautiful bottle of your rosé.

00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:41.234
-Thank you.
-And here's yours.

00:00:41.234 --> 00:00:43.400
-Why aren't
all TV chat shows like this?

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.467
-I know! Well, nobody's
polite enough to bring wine.

00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:48.534
-Cheers to you all.
-Cheers to you guys.

00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:51.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.534 --> 00:00:53.734
This is absolutely lovely.
-Mmm!

00:00:53.734 --> 00:00:55.868
-Now, I have a lot of questions.

00:00:55.868 --> 00:00:58.300
I have a lot of questions
about your wine.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.100
But I also want
to ask about this.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.300
When was this performance
with Coldplay?

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.400
It was this trip
when you were out here?

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.400
-Yeah, it was
a couple nights ago.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.934
-So you're at MetLife Stadium,

00:01:05.934 --> 00:01:09.567
and Coldplay reaches out
and asks you to do a song?

00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:11.234
-Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughs ]

00:01:11.234 --> 00:01:12.968
And what do you say?
Yes.

00:01:12.968 --> 00:01:14.601
-Wow!
That's fantastic.

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:17.133
And so you -- I mean, no one
knew you were gonna be there,

00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:20.133
and you walk out and --
-I barely knew I was gonna be.

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:23.100
This is a reach-out
the day before.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.701
And I know the guys.
I've known them for a long time.

00:01:24.701 --> 00:01:26.868
-Yes. But they just --
When the word gets to them

00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:28.234
that you're in town?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:28.234 --> 00:01:29.534
-Wow!
-But the thing is,

00:01:29.534 --> 00:01:32.167
I banned myself from seeing
the rest of the show,

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.667
because I've got
a performance myself tonight,

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:37.334
actually, and I knew if
I watched the Coldplay show,

00:01:37.334 --> 00:01:42.167
I would sing, scream, yell,
dance, ruin myself.

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:44.300
So, love Coldplay,
performed with Coldplay,

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.367
but didn't see it.
I banned myself.

00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:47.734
-Wow!
-Yeah, I'll see them

00:01:47.734 --> 00:01:51.267
another time, when I can
really let loose.

00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:53.133
-What was the inspiration

00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:56.601
for Kylie Minogue
having her own wine?

00:01:56.601 --> 00:01:58.234
-It's pretty simple, actually.

00:01:58.234 --> 00:02:00.167
I love a good glass of wine.

00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:03.200
And when I was recording
in Nashville, of all places,

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.767
a few years back,
it was peak summer, boiling hot,

00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:08.667
and Nashville
work sensible hours,

00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:10.501
sensible songwriting hours.

00:02:10.501 --> 00:02:12.868
So each night
was al fresco dining

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:14.934
in the heat
with a glass of rosé.

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:16.667
And one night,
the light was coming through it,

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:19.701
and I just said, "I'd love
to have my own rosé one day,"

00:02:19.701 --> 00:02:22.133
and a few years later, I do.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:24.868
Yeah, so it's
a pretty thrilling industry.

00:02:24.868 --> 00:02:27.167
It's all new to me.
I'm learning as I go along.

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:30.100
-And I don't think a pandemic
was good for anyone,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.501
but I feel like people
who are selling wine

00:02:31.501 --> 00:02:33.667
maybe did all right.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:39.000
-Well, weirdly, I met with
the team I work with March 2020,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.501
so we actually developed
the range

00:02:41.501 --> 00:02:42.868
on the doorstep, pretty much.

00:02:42.868 --> 00:02:45.868
My house, they'd drop off --
here's the bottles,

00:02:45.868 --> 00:02:48.300
here's the juice,
here's the labels,

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:49.968
you put them all together.

00:02:49.968 --> 00:02:52.667
And yeah, I thought,
"Does this work?"

00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:54.667
releasing a wine
at that point in time?

00:02:54.667 --> 00:02:55.767
And actually, it did.
-Yeah.

00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:57.167
-Yeah.
-There you go.

00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:58.968
I think people were looking
to have something to drink,

00:02:58.968 --> 00:03:01.934
and you were somebody
they trusted.

00:03:01.934 --> 00:03:03.968
So that's...
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:03.968 --> 00:03:08.033
-So, we have photos from your
first time in New York City.

00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:10.000
So...
-Okay, let's just get it

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.234
out of the way.
Let's deal with 1988.

00:03:12.234 --> 00:03:14.067
-All right, 1988.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:17.133
Now you have --
this is "Loco-Motion," right?

00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:18.767
-I thought you were gonna say,
"You have a perm."

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:20.133
-[ Laughs ]
Well, you have a perm.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.801
-Which I may have.
-So I think that this is --

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:25.934
this is really lovely, because
obviously, your first time here,

00:03:25.934 --> 00:03:27.834
you might have thought this was
a fancy part of the city.

00:03:27.834 --> 00:03:30.133
[ Laughter ]
Oh, look at the junk.

00:03:30.133 --> 00:03:32.634
That's amazing.
No one thought to clean that up.

00:03:32.634 --> 00:03:35.267
That's amazing.
-But now this is crazy --

00:03:35.267 --> 00:03:38.167
you have had --
in five decades,

00:03:38.167 --> 00:03:40.834
you have had a number-one hit

00:03:40.834 --> 00:03:42.901
in every decade
for the last five decades.

00:03:42.901 --> 00:03:44.434
-I've had a number-one album
in five decades.

00:03:44.434 --> 00:03:46.267
-Number-one album, excuse me.
I'm selling you short.

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:49.400
-I know.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.000
Yeah, and I guess
the weird thing is, I was --

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.868
A couple of years ago --
I was 52 --

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:57.868
so I had to do the math.
Like, okay --

00:03:57.868 --> 00:04:00.634
five decades
with five number-ones.

00:04:00.634 --> 00:04:02.400
But, you know,
it was the late '80s

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.534
to the start of the 2020s.
-Well, it's pretty amazing.

00:04:04.534 --> 00:04:06.868
-Yeah, I mean,
it's mind-blowing.

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:09.667
I didn't know
when I was that age

00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:11.734
and that girl
that I would have this...

00:04:11.734 --> 00:04:12.868
-Yeah.
-...these opportunities.

00:04:12.868 --> 00:04:14.234
-And you were like,
"Why are they putting me

00:04:14.234 --> 00:04:17.300
in whatever this machine is?"
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:19.934
-I have a real memory of you.

00:04:19.934 --> 00:04:21.267
You were the musical guest
at "SNL."

00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:22.467
It was my first year
on the show,

00:04:22.467 --> 00:04:24.100
and Ian McKellen was the host.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.534
-I didn't know
that was your first year.

00:04:25.534 --> 00:04:28.434
-And he was the happiest
I've ever seen

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:30.767
as a host introducing
a musical guest.

00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:33.234
And for years, we would --
someone would just,

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:35.934
out of nowhere say,
"Ladies and gentlemen,

00:04:35.934 --> 00:04:40.000
you lucky, lucky people,
Kylie Minogue!"

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.033
-Yeah.
-He was so happy.

00:04:42.033 --> 00:04:44.100
-I remember he was perched
on the side of the stage

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.367
just like --
How to be more nervous on "SNL."

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:50.367
Ian McKellen right there.
But he was kind of...

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:52.734
Yeah, he's very generous.
-He came by it honestly.

00:04:52.734 --> 00:04:54.868
He was genuinely excited.
It was the only time that,

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:56.634
during the song,
they cut to the host,

00:04:56.634 --> 00:04:58.968
sitting on the side of the
stage, watching, and I'm like...

00:04:58.968 --> 00:05:00.400
-Yeah. I'll take it.
That's great.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.267
-I was like,
"Kylie might have means

00:05:02.267 --> 00:05:04.000
to have
a restraining order filed."

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.834
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.834 --> 00:05:08.234
So this is -- Obviously,
you've been very successful,

00:05:08.234 --> 00:05:10.400
but this is a photo of you
with Conor McGregor.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.501
And this was very --
this helped your success

00:05:12.501 --> 00:05:16.701
with your nieces and nephews.
-Well, I have three nephews,

00:05:16.701 --> 00:05:19.000
and once in a while --

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.334
I mean,
that was a totally random --

00:05:20.334 --> 00:05:22.801
We look so happy there.
-You look so happy.

00:05:22.801 --> 00:05:25.000
This is a recent photo --
-I was happy.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.601
He looks very happy.
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:27.601 --> 00:05:29.934
-I just bumped into him
after the "Elvis" premiere

00:05:29.934 --> 00:05:31.868
in Cannes, as you do.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.868 --> 00:05:33.834
Some things roll off the tongue.
-You know, you're at Cannes,

00:05:33.834 --> 00:05:36.467
and you meet Conor McGregor.
We've all been there.

00:05:36.467 --> 00:05:43.601
-And, yes, and my three nephews
are 11, 13, and 16.

00:05:43.601 --> 00:05:45.667
Prime.
So I get the message

00:05:45.667 --> 00:05:49.300
from one of them going,
"You met Conor McGregor?"

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.167
Like, there's so many moments
that I don't think

00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:54.534
they're gonna travel that far,
but I was excited.

00:05:54.534 --> 00:05:57.100
But my nephews were peaking.
-And this is a photo --

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:58.534
I also think this is very cool.

00:05:58.534 --> 00:06:01.467
Conor McGregor asked for
the photo with you, correct?

00:06:01.467 --> 00:06:02.901
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.901 --> 00:06:04.567
-And you're --
-But given a minute,

00:06:04.567 --> 00:06:07.567
I might've asked, as well.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:09.968
-This is also cool.
-It's almost like we checked

00:06:09.968 --> 00:06:10.968
what each other
was gonna wear there.

00:06:10.968 --> 00:06:12.634
-I know.
It really does look like

00:06:12.634 --> 00:06:16.734
you arranged it in advanced.
So, this is really cool.

00:06:16.734 --> 00:06:19.067
So there's an Australian soap
called "Neighbours,"

00:06:19.067 --> 00:06:20.868
and I'm aware of the show
because I feel like

00:06:20.868 --> 00:06:25.200
almost every Australian
who has succeeded started there.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.033
-Yeah.
-And they're having a finale.

00:06:28.033 --> 00:06:30.434
This is it.
They're wrapping it up after...?

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:34.267
-After 30-something --
I don't know how -- 37 years.

00:06:34.267 --> 00:06:35.601
I don't know.
Long enough. Long enough.

00:06:35.601 --> 00:06:37.567
-This is the show that you got
your break on, right?

00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:40.567
Your first time?
-Yeah, my first kind of --

00:06:40.567 --> 00:06:43.334
I started acting
when I was 11, 16.

00:06:43.334 --> 00:06:45.801
When I was 19, I finished
school, signed up for the Dole

00:06:45.801 --> 00:06:47.100
'cause I thought,
"I'm never gonna get a job,"

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:49.567
but I got this role
on "Neighbours,"

00:06:49.567 --> 00:06:52.501
and that was in 1986.
-Wow.

00:06:52.501 --> 00:06:55.667
-So, I was only in the show
for 2 1/2 years,

00:06:55.667 --> 00:06:58.434
but it's when it just exploded.

00:06:58.434 --> 00:07:03.133
Like, you know, England,
they just went crazy about it.

00:07:03.133 --> 00:07:05.167
And so it's been with me
ever since,

00:07:05.167 --> 00:07:08.767
and it's a large part of how I
entered into the music industry.

00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:10.901
And, you know,
you're in people's households.

00:07:10.901 --> 00:07:13.734
The show was played twice a day,
actually, and people would

00:07:13.734 --> 00:07:17.100
bunk off school, they'd leave --
they'd change the office hours.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:24.267
It was a huge thing, because
whoever knew how to set the VHS?

00:07:24.267 --> 00:07:26.434
Yeah, so they're finishing
the show,

00:07:26.434 --> 00:07:28.767
and there was quite
a bit of pressure for me

00:07:28.767 --> 00:07:31.267
to go back, so I shot my scenes.
-You went back!

00:07:31.267 --> 00:07:33.033
You went back for the finale.
Oh, very cool.

00:07:33.033 --> 00:07:35.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:35.467 --> 00:07:39.501
-And another friend you may have
had on the show, Guy Pearce.

00:07:39.501 --> 00:07:40.934
-Yes.
-He was in the same show,

00:07:40.934 --> 00:07:43.367
so he's just filmed
his little return, as well.

00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:46.100
So we're all gonna cry.
-Oh, that's wonderful.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:47.567
I'm so glad you did that.
Thank you so much

00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:49.000
for bringing the wine
and being here with us today.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.334
-Thank you!
-I really appreciate it.

00:07:50.334 --> 00:07:52.067
-Nice seeing you.
-Kylie Minogue, everybody.

00:07:52.067 --> 00:07:53.400
For more information
on her wine,

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.734
visit KylieMinogueWines.com.

00:07:55.734 --> 00:07:58.801
We'll be right back
with Leila Mottley.

